    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e$4.76 a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for chicken afritada. Filipino turo-turo plates run $12 to $15 where you can find them, and in most of the Midwest you cannot, which makes your version priceless and also $4.76.",
    "shop_smart":  "Chicken thighs carry the dish at 7.5 pounds. The clamp on this recipe keeps portions honest at a still-huge 57 grams of protein.\nThe tomato base is the other big line in canned diced tomatoes. Store-brand cans are the move, and if crushed tomatoes are cheaper that week they work the same here.\nA splash of fish sauce is the authentic secret and costs almost nothing per batch. The bottle lasts a year of Filipino and Thai recipes.",
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Cut the chicken thighs into large chunks and pat dry. Brown them in batches in some of the vegetable oil in your big pot. Set aside.",
                    "Fry the diced potatoes and carrots in the remaining oil 5 minutes until they pick up color. Set aside with the chicken.",
                    "Saute the diced onion until soft, then the garlic for 1 minute.",
                    "Add the diced tomatoes and fish sauce, bring to a simmer, and cook 10 minutes to concentrate.",
                    "Return the chicken, potatoes, and carrots. Cover and simmer 20 to 25 minutes until the chicken reads 165 F and the potatoes are tender.",
                    "Stir in the sliced green and red bell peppers and the peas for the last 5 minutes so they keep some snap. Season with black pepper and salt to taste.",
                    "Portion into 14 containers, distributing chicken and vegetables evenly."
                ],
